    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220">

                <pb facs="0558" n="444" xml:id="pg444"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of War</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Presidents Special Assistant</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d220-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-04">August 4, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Harry</hi>: As a preliminary to my talk with
                    him, it may help the President to read the enclosed memorandum which I have
                    drawn up as a summary of my observations on Italy and 
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Overlord</hi>. I shall be very grateful
                    if you would read over this memorandum and, if you think it would help as a
                    guide to our conversations, ask the President to read it.</p>
                <p>The Prime Minister also asked me to speak to the President on two other subjects
                    of lesser complexity but major importance, as to one of which he has given me a
                    memorandum for the President which I can bring up.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d220-f2"
                    /> I really think the President should have all of this information before the
                    coming Quebec conference.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry L Stimson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d220-a1.xml" xpointer="d220-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d220-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d220-f1-content">
                <p>This letter and the enclosed memorandum were delivered to Hopkins in Washington
                    shortly before his departure to join Roosevelt, who was vacationing at Birch
                    Island, Ontario. Hopkins passed the memorandum on to Roosevelt, who sent word to
                    Stimson on August 5 that he had read it. After Roosevelt and Hopkins had
                    returned to Washington, Stimson discussed the memorandum with Hopkins at lunch
                    on August 9 and with Roosevelt at lunch on August 10. See Stimson and Bundy,
                        <ref target="#pg434">pp. 434</ref>–<ref target="#pg435">435</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d220-f2" n="2" xml:id="d220-f2-content">
                <p>The two subjects referred to were Anglo-American cooperation with respect to
                    atomic energy (see <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg637">p.
                        637</ref>) and the organization of anti-submarine warfare within the United
                    States armed forces.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
